e \\(y=1,2,3\\).\n\n*If \\(L_{1}=y=1\\):* then \\(L_{1}\\) contains \\((1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\).  \nThe remaining points are  \n\n\\[\nS\\setminus L_{1}= \\{(1,2),(1,3),(2,2)\\}.\n\\]\n\nAny line through two of these three points has slope  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{2-3}{1-1}=\\infty\\;( \\text{vertical}),\\qquad\n\\frac{2-2}{2-1}=0\\;( \\text{horizontal}),\\qquad\n\\frac{3-2}{1-2}=-1,\n\\]\n\nall of which are non‑sunny. Hence two sunny lines cannot cover\n\\(S\\setminus L_{1}\\).\n\n*If \\(L_{1}=y=2\\):* then \\(L_{1}\\) contains \\((1,2)\\) only.\nThe set \\(S\\setminus L_{1}\\) has five points; among any five points of \\(S\\)\nthere are two with the same \\(x\\)-coordinate or the same sum \\(x+y\\), so a\nline joining them is again non‑sunny. Consequently two sunny lines cannot\ncover the five points.\n\n*If \\(L_{1}=y=3\\):* analogous to the case \\(y=1\\) (the points \\((1,3)\\) and\n\\((3,1)\\) are interchanged). The same contradiction appears.\n\nThus a horizontal non‑sunny line is impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  \\(L_{1}\\) vertical \\((x=c)\\)\n\nThe only vertical lines meeting \\(S\\) are \\(x=1,2,3\\).\n\n*If \\(L_{1}=x=1\\):* then \\(L_{1}\\) contains \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\).  \nThe complement  \n\n\\[\nS\\setminus L_{1}= \\{(2,1),(3,1),(2,2)\\}\n\\]\n\nhas the same three points as in the horizontal case above, and any line\nthrough two of them is again non‑sunny (slopes \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).  \n\n*If \\(L_{1}=x=2\\):* then \\(L_{1}\\) contains \\((2,1),(2,2)\\).  \nThe remaining four points are \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(3,1)\\).  \nAny line joining two of these four points is either vertical,\nhorizontal or of slope \\(-1\\); a short computation of the six possible\npairs shows this explicitly. Hence two sunny lines cannot cover them.\n\n*If \\(L_{1}=x=3\\):* symmetric to the case \\(x=1\\) and leads to the same\ncontradiction.\n\nThus a vertical non‑sunny line is impossible.\n\n--------------------------------