            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_{3}=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le4\\}\n      =\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical), and \\(-1\\); i.e. it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\nWe must decide, for each integer \\(k\\ge0\\), whether there exist three distinct lines \\(L_{1},L_{2},L_{3}\\) such that  \n\n* every point of \\(S_{3}\\) lies on at least one of the three lines, and  \n* exactly \\(k\\) of the three lines are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  \\(k=0\\) (no sunny line)\n\nTake the three vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_{1}:x=1,\\qquad L_{2}:x=2,\\qquad L_{3}:x=3 .\n\\]\n\nEach line is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non‑sunny.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in S_{3}\\) we have \\(a\\in\\{1,2,3\\}\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  \nThus all six points are covered and \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  \\(k=1\\) (exactly one sunny line)\n\nChoose  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_{s}&: y=x \\qquad\\text{(slope }1\\text{, sunny)},\\\\\nL_{v}&: x=1 \\qquad\\text{(vertical, non‑sunny)},\\\\\nL_{h}&: y=1 \\qquad\\text{(horizontal, non‑sunny)} .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThe three lines are distinct. Their incidences with the points of \\(S_{3}\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\n\\text{Line} & \\text{Points of }S_{3}\\text{ it contains}\\\\ \\hline\nL_{s}&(1,1),\\;(2,2)\\\\\nL_{v}&(1,2),\\;(1,3)\\\\\nL_{h}&(2,1),\\;(3,1)\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nHence every point of \\(S_{3}\\) lies on at least one of the three lines and exactly one line (\\(L_{s}\\)) is sunny.  \nTherefore \\(k=1\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  \\(k=3\\) (all three lines sunny)\n\nConsider the three lines  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_{1}&: y-x=0 \\quad\\text{(through }(1,1),(2,2)),\\\\\nL_{2}&: y-\\tfrac12 x= \\tfrac32 \\quad\\text{(through }(1,2),(3,1)),\\\\\nL_{3}&: y-2x= -1 \\quad\\text{(through }(1,3),(2,1)).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nTheir slopes are respectively \\(1,\\; \\tfrac12,\\;2\\); none equals \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), so all three are sunny.  \nThe three pairs of points are disjoint and together constitute the whole set \\(S_{3}\\).  \nConsequently every point of \\(S_{3}\\) lies on a sunny line and \\(k=3\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  \\(k=2\\) (exactly two sunny lines) – impossibility\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that three distinct lines \\(L_{1},L_{2},L_{3}\\) cover \\(S_{3}\\) and exactly two of them are sunny.  \nHence one of the lines, say \\(L_{1}\\), is non‑sunny; therefore \\(L_{1}\\) belongs to one of the three families:\n\n* **Vertical:** \\(L_{1}:x=c\\) with \\(c\\in\\{1,2,3\\}\\);\n* **Horizontal:** \\(L_{1}:y=c\\) with \\(c\\in\\{1,2,3\\}\\);\n* **Diagonal:** \\(L_{1}:x+y=c\\) with \\(c\\in\\{2,3,4,5\\}\\).\n\nWe treat the three possibilities separately.\n\n---\n\n#### 4.1  \\(L_{1}\\) vertical, say \\(x=1\\)\n\nThen the points \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\) are already covered.  \nThe remaining points are  \n\n\\[\nR=\\{(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nA sunny line cannot contain two points having the same \\(x\\)-coordinate, the same \\(y\\)-coordinate, **or** the same sum \\(x+y\\).  \nIn the set \\(R\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c|c}\n\\text{Point}&x&y&x+y\\\\ \\hline\n(2,1)&2&1&3\\\\\n(2,2)&2&2&4\\\\\n(3,1)&3&1&4\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nThus any two points of \\(R\\) share either the same \\(x\\) (the first two) or the same \\(y\\) (the first and third) or the same sum (the last two). Consequently **no sunny line can contain two points of \\(R\\)**.  \nHence at least three sunny lines would be needed to cover \\(R\\), contradicting the assumption that only two sunny lines are available.\n\n---\n\n#### 4.2  \\(L_{1}\\) horizontal, say \\(y=1\\)\n\nNow \\((1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\) are covered, and the uncovered set is  \n\n\\[\nR=\\{(1,2),(1,3),(2,2)\\}.\n\\]\n\nTheir coordinates are  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c|c}\n\\text{Point}&x&y&x+y\\\\ \\hline\n(1,2)&1&2&3\\\\\n(1,3)&1&3&4\\\\\n(2,2)&2&2&4\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nAgain any two points of \\(R\\) share either the same \\(x\\) (first two) or the same sum (last two). Hence no sunny line can contain two points of \\(R\\), and we would need three sunny lines – a contradiction.\n\n---\n\n#### 4.3  \\(L_{1}\\) diagonal, say \\(x+y=4\\)\n\nThe line \\(x+y=4\\) contains \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\).  \nThe remaining points are  \n\n\\[\nR=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nTheir data:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c|c}\n\\text{Point}&x&y&x+y\\\\ \\hline\n(1,1)&1&1&2\\\\\n(1,2)&1&2&3\\\\\n(2,1)&2&1&3\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nAgain any two points of \\(R\\) share either the same \\(x\\) (first two) or the same sum (last two). Thus no sunny line can contain two points of \\(R\\), forcing at least three sunny lines – contradiction.\n\n---\n\nSince all three possibilities for the unique non‑sunny line lead to a contradiction, a configuration with exactly two sunny lines cannot exist. Therefore **\\(k=2\\) is impossible**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n