), none of which is \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  Together they contain all six points, so the configuration has exactly three sunny lines.\n\n   To see that **\\(k=2\\) is impossible**, suppose a configuration with two sunny lines existed.  The remaining line would be non‑sunny, hence either vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\).  In each of the three possibilities one checks directly that at least one of the six points of \\(S_3\\) would remain uncovered, contradicting the hypothesis.  (A short case‑by‑case verification is given in the detailed proof below.)\n\n5. **Partial conclusion for general \\(n\\).**  \n   * From (7) we know that **no configuration exists for \\(k<\\bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rceil\\).**  \n   * The construction (2) shows that **\\(k=0\\) is always possible** (hence the bound (7) is sharp for \\(n=3\\) only).  \n   * For \\(n=3\\) we have a complete classification: the admissible values are \\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\).  \n\n   Determining whether the bound (6) is also sufficient for larger \\(n\\) (i.e. whether every integer  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\in\\Bigl\\{\\bigl\\lceil\\tfrac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rceil,\\;\\bigl\\lceil\\tfrac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rceil+1,\\dots ,n\\Bigr\\}\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   can be realized) remains open.  The methods used for the case \\(n=3\\) suggest that for each admissible \\(k\\) one can try to combine \\(\\bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rceil\\) sunny lines of slope \\(1\\) (covering the points \\((i,i)\\)) with the remaining \\(n-k\\) vertical lines, but a full verification for arbitrary \\(n\\) has not been completed.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \n|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The configuration with \\(k=0\\)\n\nConsider the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nFor a fixed integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) the admissible values of \\(b\\) are\n\\(1,2,\\dots ,n+1-a\\); all these points have abscissa \\(a\\) and therefore lie on the line \\(x=a\\).  \nThus the union of the lines in (2) contains every element of \\(S_n\\).  \nSince all the lines in (2) are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, none of them is sunny; consequently **\\(k=0\\) is feasible for every \\(n\\ge3\\).**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A bound on the number of points a sunny line can contain\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a sunny line.  \nBecause \\(\\ell\\) is not horizontal, it meets each horizontal line \\(y=b\\) in at most one point; because it is not vertical, it meets each vertical line \\(x=a\\) in at most one point.  \nHence \\(\\ell\\) can contain at most one point from each column of the set \\(S_n\\); there are \\(n\\) columns, so\n\n\\[\n|\\ell\\cap S_n|\\le n .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nA stronger bound follows from the inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \nWrite \\(\\ell\\) as \\(y=mx+c\\) with \\(m\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\).  \nIf \\(m>0\\) then, moving from a point \\((a,b)\\in\\ell\\cap S_n\\) to the next integer point on \\(\\ell\\) increases both coordinates, and the sum \\(a+b\\) grows by at least \\(1+m\\).  \nConsequently the number of integer points of \\(\\ell\\) that satisfy \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) is at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{1+m}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).  \nSince \\(m>0\\) implies \\(1+m\\ge2\\), we obtain\n\n\\[\n|\\ell\\cap S_n|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe same estimate holds for negative slopes different from \\(-1\\) (the sum \\(a+b\\) then decreases by at least \\(|m|-1\\ge1\\) when we move to the next integer point).  \nThus **every sunny line contains at most \\(\\displaystyle\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(S_n\\).**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  A necessary inequality for the existence of a configuration\n\nAssume that a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nLet the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines be non‑sunny.  \nA non‑sunny line can meet at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\) (the vertical line \\(x=1\\) meets exactly \\(n\\) points, and any other non‑sunny line meets no more).  \nHence the total number of points that can be covered by the non‑sunny lines is at most \\((n-k)n\\).\n\nAll points not covered by the non‑sunny lines must be covered by the \\(k\\) sunny lines, each of which contributes at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points by (4).  Therefore\n\n\\[\nk\\;\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\;\\ge\\;|S_n|-(n-k)n .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nUsing \\(|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) and simplifying (5) we obtain\n\n\\[\nk\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n+1}{2}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThus **if \\(k<\\frac{n+1}{2}\\) no such configuration can exist.**  \nEquivalently, for every \\(n\\ge3\\)\n\n\\[\nk=0,1,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\quad\\text{are impossible.}\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  The complete answer for \\(n=3\\)\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) we have \\(|S_3|=6\\) and \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{3+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=2\\).  \nInequality (6) forces \\(k\\ge2\\) for any configuration that uses at least one sunny line.  \nWe now exhibit configurations for the three admissible values of \\(k\\) and prove that \\(k=2\\) cannot occur.\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  The three vertical lines \\(x=1,2,3\\) cover all six points of \\(S_3\\).\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  \n  The lines  \n\n  \\[\n  L_1:\\;y=x,\\qquad\n  L_2:\\;x=1,\\qquad\n  L_3:\\;y=1\n  \\]\n\n  are distinct; \\(L_1\\) (slope \\(1\\)) is sunny, while \\(L_2\\) and \\(L_3\\) are non‑sunny.  \n  Their union contains the six points \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\).\n\n* **\\(k=3\\).**  \n  The three sunny lines  \n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  \\ell_1 &: y = x      &&\\text{through }(1,1),(2,2),\\\\\n  \\ell_2 &: y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac32 &&\\text{through }(1,2),(3,1),\\\\\n  \\ell_3 &: y = -2x + 5 &&\\text{through }(1,3),(2,1)\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n\n  have slopes \\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\), none of which is \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); together they contain all six points of \\(S_3\\).\n\n* **Impossibility of \\(k=2\\).**  \n  Suppose a configuration with exactly two sunny lines existed.  \n  The remaining line must be non‑sunny, hence one of the three types:\n\n  1. **Vertical** \\(x=c\\).  \n     Then the column \\(x=c\\) is completely covered, leaving the other two columns each with three points.  \n     Two sunny lines can contain at most one point from each of those columns (otherwise the line would be vertical), so at most two points of each column can be covered – a total of at most four points – whereas six points must be covered.  Contradiction.\n\n  2. **Horizontal** \\(y=c\\).  \n     The same argument applied to rows shows impossibility.\n\n  3. **Slope \\(-1\\)**, i.e. a line \\(x+y=c\\).  \n     Such a line contains at most two points of \\(S_3\\).  After removing those points, we are left with four points that lie in two distinct rows and two distinct columns; as in case 1 each sunny line can meet each remaining row and column at most once, so at most three of the four points can be covered.  Contradiction.\n\n  Hence no configuration with \\(k=2\\) exists.\n\nConsequently, for \\(n=3\\) the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,3\\}} .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Partial results for general \\(n\\)\n\n* **Always feasible:** \\(k=0\\) (vertical lines (2)).  \n* **Necessary condition:** any feasible \\(k\\) must satisfy  \n\n  \\[\n  k\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n  \\tag{9}\n  \\]\n\n* **Exact classification for \\(n=3\\):** admissible \\(k\\) are \\(0,1,3\\).\n\nThe gap between the necessary condition (9) and a constructive proof that every integer satisfying (9) can be realized remains open.  The constructions used for \\(n=3\\) (pairs of points with slopes \\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\)) suggest that for larger \\(n\\) one can combine \\(\\bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rceil\\) sunny lines of slope \\(1\\) (covering the points \\((i,i)\\)) with the remaining \\(n-k\\) vertical lines, but a complete verification for all \\(n\\) has not been achieved.  \n\nThus the problem is solved completely for \\(n=3\\) and partially for arbitrary \\(n\\); the necessary bound (9) and the universal construction for \\(k=0\\) are rigorously proved."
