    \\]\n     The slope is \\(1\\), so every \\(\\ell_t\\) is sunny.\n     For a point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have\n     \\(|a-b|\\le m\\) (because \\(a+b\\le2m+2\\) and \\(a,b\\ge1\\)),\n     hence \\(b=a+t\\) for the unique \\(t=a-b\\) with \\(|t|\\le m\\); thus\n     \\((a,b)\\) lies on exactly one of the lines \\(\\ell_t\\).\n     Consequently the \\(n\\) lines \\(\\{\\ell_t\\mid t=-m,\\dots ,m\\}\\) cover\n     all points of \\(S_n\\) and are all sunny, giving a configuration with\n     \\(k=n\\).\n\n   * **\\(k=n-1\\) when \\(n\\) is even.**  \n     Write \\(n=2m\\) (\\(m\\ge2\\)).  Consider the \\(2m-1\\) sunny lines\n     \\[\n     \\ell_t:\\; y = x + t ,\\qquad t=-m+1,\\dots ,m-1 .\n     \\tag{11}\n     \\]\n     As in the odd case, every point \\((a,b)\\) with\n     \\(|a-b|\\le m-1\\) lies on exactly one of these lines.\n     The only points of \\(S_n\\) not yet covered are those with\n     \\(a-b=m\\) or \\(a-b=-m\\); they are precisely\n     \\[\n     (m+1,1),\\;(m+2,2),\\dots ,(2m, m) \\quad\\text{and}\\quad\n     (1,m+1),(2,m+2),\\dots ,(m,2m) .\n     \\tag{12}\n     \\]\n     All these points lie on the single non‑sunny diagonal line\n     \\[\n     x+y = 2m+1 .\n     \\tag{13}\n     \\]\n     Adding this line to the family (11) yields exactly \\(2m-1+1=n\\) distinct\n     lines, of which the \\(2m-1\\) lines (11) are sunny and the line (13) is\n     non‑sunny.  Hence we obtain a configuration with \\(k=n-1\\).\n\n6. **Impossibility of \\(k=n\\) for even \\(n\\).**  \n   If \\(n=2m\\) were even and all \\(n\\) lines were sunny, then by (3) each\n   line would contain at most \\(s=m\\) points, so the total number of points\n   that could be covered would be at most \\(nm = 2m\\cdot m = n^2/2\\).\n   However \\(|S_n| = n(n+1)/2 = n^2/2 + n/2 > n^2/2\\), a contradiction.\n   Therefore the bound (7) is sharp: for even \\(n\\) one cannot have\n   \\(k=n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion.**  \n\n*For every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are*\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}, & n\\ \\text{odd},\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}, & n\\ \\text{even}.\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\nThe lower end \\(k=0\\) is realised by the vertical‑line construction (9);\nthe upper end is realised by the families (10) and (11)–(13).  \nThe necessary inequality (8) shows that no other values of \\(k\\) are\npossible, completing the classification."
