d q\\); consequently among the \\(n\\) possible\n   abscissae \\(1,2,\\dots ,n\\) at most \\(\\bigl\\lceil n/q\\bigr\\rceil\\) satisfy\n   the congruence, and therefore \\(\\ell\\) contains at most \\(\\bigl\\lceil n/q\\bigr\\rceil\\)\n   points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n   The bound is strongest when \\(q=1\\), i.e. when the slope is an integer\n   \\(m\\neq0,-1\\).  For such a line \\(y=mx+c\\) with integer \\(c\\) we have\n   \\[\n   a+ma+c\\le n+1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n   a\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-c}{1+m}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n   The right‑hand side is maximised by the choice \\(c=0\\) (or \\(c=-1\\) when\n   \\(m<0\\)), giving\n   \\[\n   |\\ell\\cap S_n|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   Hence **every sunny line contains at most**\n   \\[\n   s:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   \\]\n   points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n3. **Maximum number of points on a non‑sunny line.**  \n   A line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or to \\(x+y=0\\) meets\n   \\(S_n\\) in at most \\(n\\) points (the vertical line \\(x=1\\) meets exactly\n   \\(n\\) points, and any other line of this type meets no more).  Thus\n   \\[\n   \\text{if }\\ell\\text{ is non‑sunny, then }|\\ell\\cap S_n|\\le n .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n4. **A necessary inequality for the number of sunny lines.**  \n   Suppose a configuration uses exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny\n   lines.  The total number of points that can be covered is at most\n   \\[\n   (n-k)\\,n\\;+\\;k\\,s .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n   Since all \\(|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) points must be covered, (5) yields\n   \\[\n   (n-k)n+k s\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting \\(s=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) and solving for\n   \\(k\\) gives\n\n   * if \\(n\\) is odd, \\(s=\\frac{n+1}{2}\\) and (6) is satisfied for every\n     \\(k\\le n\\);\n   * if \\(n\\) is even, \\(s=\\frac{n}{2}\\) and (6) simplifies to\n     \\[\n     k\\le n-1 .\n     \\tag{7}\n     \\]\n\n   Hence **a necessary condition** is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;k\\le n\\;(n\\text{ odd}),\\qquad k\\le n-1\\;(n\\text{ even})\\;}\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Extremal constructions.**\n\n   * **\\(k=0\\) for every \\(n\\).**  \n     The \\(n\\) vertical lines\n     \\[\n     x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n\n     \\tag{9}\n     \\]\n     are all non‑sunny and together contain every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n   * **\\(k=n\\) when \\(n\\) is odd.**  \n     Write \\(n=2m+1\\) (\\(m\\ge1\\)).  For each integer\n     \\(t\\in\\{-m,-m+1,\\dots ,m\\}\\) define the line\n     \\[\n     \\ell_t:\\; y = x + t .\n     \\tag{10}\n     \\]\n     The slope is \\(1\\), so every \\(\\ell_t\\) is sunny.\n     For a point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have\n     \\(|a-b|\\le m\\) (because \\(a+b\\le2m+2\\) and \\(a,b\\ge1\\)),\n     hence \\(b=a+t\\) for the unique \\(t=a-b\\) with \\(|t|\\le m\\); thus\n     \\((a,b)\\) lies on exactly one of the lines \\(\\ell_t\\).\n     Consequently the \\(n\\) lines \\(\\{\\ell_t\\mid t=-m,\\dots ,m\\}\\) cover\n     all points of \\(S_n\\) and are all sunny, giving a configuration with\n     \\(k=n\\).\n\n   * **\\(k=n-1\\) when \\(n\\) is even.**  \n     Write \\(n=2m\\) (\\(m\\ge2\\)).  Consider the \\(2m-1\\) sunny lines\n     \\[\n     \\ell_t:\\; y = x + t ,\\qquad t=-m+1,\\dots ,m-1 .\n     \\tag{11}\n     \\]\n     As in the odd case, every point \\((a,b)\\) with\n     \\(|a-b|\\le m-1\\) lies on exactly one of these lines.\n     The only points of \\(S_n\\) not yet covered are those with\n     \\(a-b=m\\) or \\(a-b=-m\\); they are precisely\n     \\[\n     (m+1,1),\\;(m+2,2),\\dots ,(2m, m) \\quad\\text{and}\\quad\n     (1,m+1),(2,m+2),\\dots ,(m,2m) .\n     \\tag{12}\n     \\]\n     All these points lie on the single non‑sunny diagonal line\n     \\[\n     x+y = 2m+1 .\n     \\tag{13}\n     \\]\n     Adding this line to the family (11) yields exactly \\(2m-1+1=n\\) distinct\n     lines, of which the \\(2m-1\\) lines (11) are sunny and the line (13) is\n     non‑sunny.  Hence we obtain a configuration with \\(k=n-1\\).\n\n6. **Impossibility of \\(k=n\\) for even \\(n\\).**  \n   If \\(n=2m\\) were even and all \\(n\\) lines were sunny, then by (3) each\n   line would contain at most \\(s=m\\) points, so the total number of points\n   that could be covered would be at most \\(nm = 2m\\cdot m = n^2/2\\).\n   However \\(|S_n| = n(n+1)/2 = n^2/2 + n/2 > n^2/2\\), a contradiction.\n   Therefore the bound (7) is sharp: for even \\(n\\) one cannot have\n   \\(k=n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion.**  \n\n*For every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are*\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}, & n\\ \\text{odd},\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}, & n\\ \\text{even}.\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\nThe lower end \\(k=0\\) is realised by the vertical‑line construction (9);\nthe upper end is realised by the families (10) and (11)–(13).  \nThe necessary inequality (8) shows that no other values of \\(k\\) are\npossible, completing the classification.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
