uently **every sunny line contains at most \\(s\\) points of\n   \\(S_n\\).**\n\n3. **Maximum size of a non\u2011sunny line.**  \n   A line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or to \\(x+y=0\\) meets\n   each column (or each row, or each anti\u2011diagonal) in at most \\(n\\) points,\n   and the bound \\(n\\) is attained (e.g. the vertical line \\(x=1\\) or the\n   diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\)).  Hence a non\u2011sunny line contains at most \\(n\\)\n   points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n4. **A necessary inequality for the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines.**  \n   If exactly \\(k\\) of the \\(n\\) lines are sunny, then using the bounds of\n   \u00a7\u00a7\u202f2\u20133 we have  \n\n   \\[\n   |S_n|\\le k\\,s+(n-k)\\,n .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting \\(|S_n|=n(n+1)/2\\) and \\(s=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\)\n   gives\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{ll}\n   n=2m\\;( \\text{even}) : & k\\le n-1,\\\\[2mm]\n   n=2m+1\\;(\\text{odd}) : & k\\le n .\n   \\end{array}\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Impossibility of \\(k=n\\).**  \n   Assume that all \\(n\\) lines are sunny.  \n   *When \\(n\\) is even*, (7) already forbids \\(k=n\\).  \n\n   *When \\(n=2m+1\\) is odd*, we have \\(s=m+1\\) and\n   \\(|S_n|=n s\\).  Hence every sunny line must contain exactly \\(s\\)\n   points and the \\(n\\) lines must be pairwise disjoint.  By the discussion\n   in \u00a7\u202f2 the only sunny lines that can contain \\(s\\) points are the lines\n   of slope\u202f\\(1\\).  For a line \\(y=x+t\\) with integer \\(t\\) the number of\n   points of \\(S_n\\) it contains equals  \n\n   \\[\n   N(t)=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-t}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor-\n          \\max\\{1,\\,1-t\\}+1 .\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   A short computation shows that \\(N(t)=s\\) holds **only for**\n   \\(t=0\\); for every other integer \\(t\\) we have \\(N(t)\\le s-1\\).\n   Consequently at most one sunny line can contain the maximal number\n   \\(s\\) of points, contradicting the requirement that all \\(n\\) sunny\n