            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a complete solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nand no configuration with \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\) exists.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **A bound for a sunny line.**  \n   A sunny line has equation \\(y=mx+q\\) with \\(m\\neq0,\\;m\\neq-1\\).  \n   Write \\(m=\\frac{p}{q}\\) in lowest terms (\\(p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\;q>0,\\;\\gcd(p,q)=1\\)).  \n   For an integer abscissa \\(x\\) the ordinate is integer iff \\(x\\equiv x_{0}\\pmod q\\) for a suitable residue \\(x_{0}\\).  \n   Hence among the \\(n\\) possible abscissae \\(1,\\dots ,n\\) at most \\(\\lceil n/q\\rceil\\) points of \\(S_n\\) can lie on the line.  \n   The condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) gives \\(y\\le n+1-x\\); together with \\(y=mx+q\\) this yields  \n\n   \\[\n   (|p|+1)x\\le n+1-q .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   From (2) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\#\\bigl\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\text{ on the line}\\bigr\\}\n   \\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{|p|+1}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Since a sunny line is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\), we have \\(|p|\\ge1\\) and \\(|p|\\neq q\\).  \n   The worst case for (3) is \\(|p|=1\\); then the bound becomes  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\; \\text{any sunny line contains at most } \n          \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor \\text{ points of }S_n\\; } .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n3. **A bound for a non‑sunny line.**  \n   A non‑sunny line is either vertical (\\(x=c\\)), horizontal (\\(y=c\\)) or has slope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=c\\)).  \n   In each case it meets the set \\(S_n\\) in at most \\(n\\) points, and this bound is attained (e.g. \\(x=1\\) or \\(y=1\\) or \\(x+y=n+1\\)).  \n\n4. **Impossibility of \\(k\\ge n-1\\).**  \n   Suppose a configuration uses exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.  \n   Using the bounds (4) and the bound \\(n\\) for non‑sunny lines we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}2 =|S_n|\n   \\le k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+(n-k)\\,n .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   For \\(k=n-1\\) the right–hand side equals  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-1)\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+ n\n   \\le (n-1)\\frac{n+1}{2}+n\n   =\\frac{n^{2}+n+2}{2}<\\frac{n(n+1)}2 ,\n   \\]\n\n   a contradiction.  \n   Hence no configuration can have \\(k\\ge n-1\\). In particular \\(k=n\\) is impossible.\n\n5. **Construction for every \\(k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   We give an explicit family of configurations with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines for any\n   \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n   *Step 5.1 – a base covering.*  \n   Consider the following \\(n\\) distinct lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{1},\\dots ,L_{n-2}:\\;x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-2,\n   \\qquad\n   H_{1}:\\;y=1,\\qquad\n   H_{2}:\\;y=2 .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   The \\(n-2\\) vertical lines cover all points of \\(S_n\\) whose abscissa is\n   \\(1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\).  \n   The two horizontal lines cover the three points that lie in the two right‑most columns,\n   namely  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-1,1),\\;(n-1,2),\\;(n,1).\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus