ality holds when \\(n\\) is even,\n   strict inequality when \\(n\\) is odd), we can split \\(R\\) into\n   \\(n-1\\) (possibly empty) blocks \\(B_{1},\\dots ,B_{n-1}\\) each containing\n   at most \\(m\\) points.\n\n   *Step 3 – sunny lines for the blocks.*  \n   For each non‑empty block \\(B_{i}\\) choose any two distinct points of the\n   block; the line through them has a rational slope \\(p/q\\) with\n   \\(|p|\\ge1\\) and \\(p\\neq -q\\), hence it is sunny.  Because a line is\n   uniquely determined by two points, we obtain a sunny line \\(L_{i}\\) that\n   contains the whole block \\(B_{i}\\).  If a block consists of a single point,\n   we may draw through that point any sunny line that does not coincide with a\n   previously used line (for instance a line of slope \\(1\\) passing through\n   the point).  Thus we obtain \\(n-1\\) distinct sunny lines\n   \\(L_{1},\\dots ,L_{n-1}\\) covering all points of \\(R\\).\n\n   *Step 4 – adjusting the number of sunny lines.*  \n   The construction above yields exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.  To obtain a\n   configuration with fewer sunny lines we simply replace some of the\n   sunny lines by additional non‑sunny lines (vertical or horizontal) that\n   pass through the same points.  Because a vertical or horizontal line can\n   contain any prescribed set of points whose abscissae (resp. ordinates) are\n   equal, we may replace any chosen subset of the sunny lines by distinct\n   non‑sunny lines without destroying the covering property.  Consequently,\n   for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) we obtain a family of\n   \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny ones and covering all\n   points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   Inequality (8) shows that \\(k\\ge n\\) is impossible; the explicit\n   construction of § 5 shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n   can be realised.  Hence the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,