tly \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\), namely\n   \\((1,b)\\;(1\\le b\\le n)\\).\n\n   *Step\u202f2 \u2013 covering the remaining points.*  \n   The set \\(R=S_n\\setminus L_{0}\\) has cardinality  \n\n   \\[\n   |R|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2-n=\\frac{n(n-1)}2 .\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   Because of (3),\n\n   \\[\n   (n-1)\\,m\\;\\ge\\;|R|\n   \\qquad\\text{(equality when \\(n\\) is even).}\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence we can partition \\(R\\) into at most \\(n-1\\) blocks,\n   each block containing at most \\(m\\) points.\n\n   *Step\u202f3 \u2013 sunny lines for the blocks.*  \n   For each non\u2011empty block \\(B\\) choose any two distinct points of \\(B\\);\n   the line through them has rational slope \\(p/q\\) with\n   \\(|p|+|q|\\ge2\\) and \\(p\\neq -q\\), therefore it is sunny.\n   Because a line is uniquely determined by two points, that line contains\n   **all** points of the block \\(B\\).  Doing this for every block yields a\n   family \\(\\{L_{1},\\dots ,L_{t}\\}\\) of distinct sunny lines, where\n   \\(t\\le n-1\\), that together cover the whole set \\(R\\).\n\n   *Step\u202f4 \u2013 obtaining exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.*  \n   The construction of Steps\u202f1\u20133 gives a configuration with\n   \\(t\\) sunny lines, where \\(t\\) can be any integer between\n   \\(0\\) and \\(n-1\\) (choose the partition of \\(R\\) accordingly).\n   If we need fewer than \\(t\\) sunny lines, we simply replace some of the\n   sunny lines by additional non\u2011sunny vertical lines:\n   each block that we replace is split into its constituent columns,\n   and each column is covered by a distinct vertical line\n   \\(x=c\\) (which is non\u2011sunny).  Because a column contains at most \\(n\\)\n   points, the total number of lines never exceeds \\(n\\).\n   Consequently, for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) we can\n   produce a family of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines containing precisely\n   \\(k\\) sunny ones and covering all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n6. **Con