 of lattice points on a non\u2011sunny line.**  \n   A non\u2011sunny line is either vertical (\\(x=c\\)), horizontal (\\(y=c\\)) or of slope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=c\\)).  In each case the intersection with the triangular set \\(S_n\\) consists of at most \\(n\\) points, and this bound is attained (e.g. \\(x=1\\), \\(y=1\\) or \\(x+y=n+1\\)).  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\text{any non\u2011sunny line meets }S_n\\text{ in at most }n\\text{ points.}}\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Upper bound for the number of sunny lines.**  \n   Suppose a configuration uses exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines.  \n   Using (3) and (4),\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}2=|S_n|\n   \\le k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+(n-k)n .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Solving (5) gives \\(k\\le n-1\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\).  Hence\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{k\\le n-1.}\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Extremal constructions.**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  \n   Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Each line \\(L_i\\) contains exactly the points \\((i,b)\\) with\n   \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\); together they cover the whole set \\(S_n\\).  \n   All the lines are non\u2011sunny, so \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n   *\\(k=n-1\\).*  \n   Choose the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines of slope\u202f\\(1\\)\n\n   \\[\n   M_j:\\;y=x-(j-1)\\qquad(j=1,2,\\dots ,n-1)\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   (i.e. the lines \\(y=x,\\,y=x-1,\\dots ,y=x-(n-2)\\)).  \n   Each line \\(M_j\\) contains exactly the points of \\(S_n\\) whose\n   difference \\(a-b=j-1\\); consequently the union of the \\(n-1\\) lines\n   covers all points of \\(S_n\\) except the single point \\((n,1)\\).  \n   Add the vertical line \\(x=n\\) (non\u2011sunny) to cover that remaining point.\n   The resulting family consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(n-1\\) of\n   which are sunny, so \\(k=n-1\\) is attainable.\n\n6. **Obtaining the intermediate values.**  \n   Start from the configuration for \\(k=n-1\\) described in (5).  \n   Pick any sunny line \\(M_j\\) and any point \\(P\\) on it.  \n   Replace \\(M_j\\) by a *new* sunny line \\(M'_j\\) that passes through \\(P\\) and has an irrational slope (hence it meets no other lattice point of \\(S_n\\)).  \n   The point \\(P\\) is still covered, while all the other points that were on \\(M_j\\) become uncovered.  \n   Those uncovered points all lie in the same column (the column of \\(P\\)), and they can be covered by a single vertical line, which is non\u2011sunny.  \n   Thus we have removed one sunny line and added one non\u2011sunny line, keeping the total number of lines equal to \\(n\\) while decreasing the number of sunny lines by exactly\u202f1.  \n\n   Repeating this \u201creplacement\u2019\u2019 operation any number of times we can turn the configuration with \\(k=n-1\\) sunny lines into configurations with any prescribed number\n   \\(k\\) of sunny lines, where \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).  At each step the\n   distinctness of the lines is preserved because the newly introduced\n   vertical line has a column that was not used by any other vertical line\n   (the column of the removed sunny line).  \n\n   Hence every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) can be realised.\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   The counting argument (6) shows that no configuration can have \\(k\\ge n\\).  \n   The constructions above exhibit configurations for every admissible\n   value \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).  Therefore the set of all possible\n   numbers of sunny lines is exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{\\,0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-1\\,\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n   This completes the solution."

